ations of people you mean literal  like biological cultures  right yeah thank you yeah and  and i think it affects people i think  during a meal having  eating these biological cultures  uh is affecting people almost to a place  where people feel  euphoric in a beautiful way yeah and i  think that's what jeff is  is sort of talking about and we don't  even fully understand  i think michael pollan said that you  know  we know the body so well uh many parts  of the body we know so well but actually  what's happening in our gut our our  knowledge  is is so limited right it's like um  so i think there there's a lot of work  that's still yeah it's  gonna happen yeah it is this uh amazing  amalgam of all these different  influences and cultures you mentioned  um the japanese tradition buddhist  temple food  ideas from india china nouvelle cuisine  from france  yeah in the 70s boule patterson  passard like all of these influences  being kind of hybridized and  um uniquely uh kind of  through your you know unique perspective  creating something new out of all of  that  the host last night was um sharing about  the the grinding of the the uh the  sesame seeds every day  yeah and how that's almost become it's  like a team activity right but it's  almost like a group  meditation that begins every day with  this laborious process  oh it's amazing yeah  and like how about like the people that  have to chop the  the cucumber and the daikon yeah that  one dish  they spend all day just doing that right  for that one  dish yeah it's extremely labor intensive  but there's something about that that's  beautiful it's like so much  heart is required to do that you know  when you take it in  there is some i think there is something  intangible about  like that that is impactful one  one of um the buddhist monks  who was with us for for this last year  in new york uh his name is toshio  and he said he thinks that we should  have the guests  also grand session before their meetings  should